// Local development against sibling checkouts of loon, loon-plugins and
// loon-baseline.
//
// Copy this to go.work (which is gitignored) when you are changing those
// repositories alongside this one:
//
//	cp go.work.example go.work
//
// With it in place, `go build` and `go test` use your working copies instead
// of the versions in go.mod, so a change in loon-plugins is visible here
// immediately and without a commit, a push or a `go get`.
//
// Without it — which is the default, and what a fresh clone gets — everything
// resolves through go.mod like any other dependency, so the project builds on
// its own. That is the point: `git clone && docker compose up --build` must
// work for somebody who has never heard of the sibling repositories.
//
// The container build sets GOWORK=off explicitly, so a go.work sitting in your
// tree cannot silently change what is inside the image.
//
// Remember that go.work overrides go.mod ENTIRELY for these modules: if you
// take a dependency on something new in a sibling, `go get` it here too before
// pushing, or CI (which has no go.work) will fail on a version that only
// exists on your machine.

go 1.26.4

use (
	.
	../loon
	../loon-baseline
	../loon-plugins
)
